22:04:08 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: # Why Ears Clog on Planes and Mountains but Not Elevators The answer comes down to **rate of altitude change** and the resulting **rate of air pressure change**. ## The Mechanism Your middle ear is an air-filled cavity connected to the back of your throat by the **Eustachian tube**. This tube normally equalizes pressure between your middle ear and the outside atmosphere. When outside pressure changes faster than your Eustachian tube can adjust, you feel that clogged/popped sensation. ## Speed Makes the Difference **Planes and mountain driving:** - A plane climbs to cruising altitude (~30,000+ feet) in just minutes - Driving up a mountain can involve thousands of feet of elevation change over a relatively short time - This creates a *rapid* pressure differential between your middle ear and the outside environment - Your Eustachian tube can't equalize fast enough, so pressure builds up, causing that clogged feeling **Elevators:** - Even tall building elevators only travel a few hundred feet at most (the tallest buildings are under half a mile high) - This is a tiny fraction of the altitude change you experience in a plane - The actual pressure change is so small that your Eustachian tube easily keeps up in real-time - You essentially don't accumulate enough pressure differential to notice ## The Real Culprit: Magnitude + Speed Combined It's really a combination of two factors: 1. **How much** the elevation changes (planes: miles; elevators: hundreds of feet) 2. **How fast** that change happens A slow, gradual altitude gain (like a leisurely hike) might not cause ear popping even over a large elevation change, because your Eustachian tube has time to continuously equalize. But quick significant changes—whether from rapid ascent in a plane or a fast mountain drive—overwhelm your ear's ability to keep pace.
